A||||9||saleenfiero||HELP CAR WON'T START (don't worry i don't always type in caps)||||2||
Z||000000||saleenfiero||11-18-2000||01:00 PM||||Yeah, uh huh.  Okay, here goes.  I'm adjusting my stereo cables on my car, I have the piece of plastic near the rocker panel off just to check things out.  Anyways, I try to start my car and HUH, the thing don't start.  Just the clicking in the back of the car.  Voltage gauge in car shows I got juice (for what its worth) and everything seems fine.  Does this sound like an innocent dead battery (which I tried to quick charge, didn't work) or something much, much more sinister.  Remember, everything is fine no fuses blown, fuel pump pressurizes, all electrical works fine including amped out stereo.  Sorry for the vauge descrip. but I can't figure it out...<P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||2
Z||000001||JohnnyK||11-18-2000||01:02 PM||||If you hear the clicking, then it's probably a dead battery (Or you killed the starter, but I doubt that)||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-18-2000||01:08 PM||||battery or cables are most likely. may have lost the alternator and it is undercharging.||||reg||1
Z||000003||jerpro||11-18-2000||02:20 PM||||check for ground cables. If there are any loose touching the chassis<P>------------------<BR>Only try your best, and if you don't succeed... TRY HARDER.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||11-18-2000||02:42 PM||||But if the solonoid is clicking, i'd say it's getting power though.||||reg||1
Z||000005||deceler8||11-18-2000||03:12 PM||||Has it snowed recently ?<P>We had a snow here the other day and it was light and wind blown enough that it filled in the space between the decklid and the battery.<P>And my car wouldn't start, even though everything else seemed to work.<P>My guess is the snow caused a small voltage drain between the battery terminals...enough combined with the cold weather to run the battery down enough that it wouldn't start.<P>And I'm assuming this because it only took 15 minutes with the charger before it would start and it's started ever since.<P>Try cleaning the battery terminals and battery case. Use some grease on the terminals before you reconnect them. Side terminal batteries are more likely to have problems due to corrosion or moisture than the old fashioned post type batteries.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Rednax Asbestos||11-18-2000||09:38 PM||||Hi,<P>Check the switch on the clutch pedal. I had the same problem. Worked on my stereo and it wouldnt start anymore. Everything worked fine except the engine wouldn't turn over. Adjusted the switch and everything worked fine.||||reg||14
Z||000007||JohnnyK||11-19-2000||06:15 PM||||But if the starter is clicking, then the neutral start switch (on the clutch pedal) is working.||||reg||1
Z||000008||polomeno||11-20-2000||03:27 AM||||Is it your starter or your selonoid that is clicking?  if it's your selonoid may be a problem with the starter itself, if it's a standard try to push start it .. could also try tapping your starter itself with a wrench a few times incase it got stuck... ||||reg||1
Z||000009||ThatRickGuy||11-20-2000||11:28 AM||||I'd go with the dead battery/cables/connection with it just being a coinsadent for timing. try jump starting it, if it still clicks after that, then my guess would be check those wires you were playing w/ for the sterio, a dead short could also pull this off, just make sure you check your wiring before you go out and blow $100+ on the starting system just to find out it was a loose wire grounding out. (Like I did  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  )||||reg||1
